1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:18,552
The saved Weekend 10 outline places capacity, informed consent,
refusal, de-escalation, severe agitation, prone-positioning hazards,
monitoring and palliative issues into class practice. This lesson
converts those objectives into original rehearsal cases.

2
00:00:18,552 --> 00:00:30,840
These are practice prompts, not school assessment answers. Perform
skills, role-play and any physical procedure only under the
instructor’s supervision and the school’s competency requirements.

3
00:00:30,840 --> 00:00:46,368
Set up a learner, partner and observer. Use the original cases already
introduced in this lesson. The observer reveals the next change only
after the learner has stated the current plan, so the exercise tests
updating rather than hindsight.

4
00:00:46,368 --> 00:00:58,944
Start with roles, safety, immediate assessment and permission for the
proposed interaction. Speak the plan to the patient rather than
presenting a monologue to the examiner while ignoring the person.

5
00:00:58,944 --> 00:01:13,392
Assign one task with an expected report, then use the report to revise
the plan. If a necessary finding is missing, ask for it or state the
limit; do not have the observer supply a normal result to make the
script easier.

6
00:01:13,392 --> 00:01:24,024
Use the school rubric if available. This original structure is a
learning aid, not an invented marking scheme or a substitute for
supervised skill assessment.

7
00:01:24,024 --> 00:01:37,848
British Columbia’s Health Care Consent and Care Facility Admission Act
presumes an adult capable unless the applicable legal test is not met.
The test is tied to the decision, not to whether the clinician agrees
with the choice.

8
00:01:37,848 --> 00:01:57,288
A diagnosis, intoxication, distress, disability or communication
difference does not by itself settle capacity. Section Seven concerns
understanding the proposed-care information and understanding that it
applies to this adult’s situation; section Eight requires appropriate
communication.

9
00:01:57,288 --> 00:02:09,720
The school asks learners to explore Understanding, Appreciation,
Reasoning and Communication. Use these as prompts to elicit the
person’s own account and identify support needs.

10
00:02:09,720 --> 00:02:29,064
The four domains are not a statutory score. Section Seven asks about
the information specified in section Six, paragraph E, and its
application to the adult’s own situation. Discuss risks and
alternatives, but do not misquote the Act as a separate foreseeable-
consequences formula.

11
00:02:29,064 --> 00:02:40,416
Use plain language and an interpreter or communication aid when
needed. Give the person time when the clinical situation allows,
invite questions and avoid arguing or threatening.

12
00:02:40,416 --> 00:02:57,288
If the patient continues to refuse, follow current BCEHS A09 and local
service direction. Consultation may be required. A capable adult may
refuse for reasons the clinician would not choose, including where
serious consequences are foreseeable.

13
00:02:57,288 --> 00:03:11,232
Name the specific proposal and what was explained. A refusal of
transport is not a refusal of every examination, and an agreement to
one observation is not agreement to all future interventions.

14
00:03:11,232 --> 00:03:24,696
Record the communication support and its effect. If an interpreter or
hearing support was unavailable, document that limit and what was done
next rather than making the record read as though communication was
complete.

15
00:03:24,696 --> 00:03:38,472
Use relevant patient words and observed responses to show the
reasoning. Separate the patient’s fear from your clinical concern, and
record uncertainty honestly instead of converting disagreement into
incapacity.

16
00:03:38,472 --> 00:03:51,408
Close with the actual plan, required consultation and follow-up
responsibility. A signature and a phone number cannot repair missing
assessment or a contingency that the person cannot use.

17
00:03:51,408 --> 00:04:04,464
A 74-year-old is found by a building caretaker after a fall. The
patient answers questions but repeats one question, reports a headache
and declines transport because hospitals feel frightening.

18
00:04:04,464 --> 00:04:24,696
Do not use orientation alone as a capacity shortcut. Assess injury and
medical threats, explain the concern and recommendation, ask the
patient to describe options and likely consequences, address the fear
and communication needs, consult the current pathway, and document the
person’s own choice.

19
00:04:24,696 --> 00:04:59,472
For a vehicle collision, reconstruct the patient mechanism rather than
collecting dramatic scenery. Establish which vehicle and position,
number and type of vehicles, reported speed, exterior damage and
intrusion, restraints/airbags, initial patient condition and any loss
of consciousness. Ask about other occupants and a death in the same
vehicle when relevant. Record whether a fact is reported, witnessed or
directly seen; vehicle damage alone neither diagnoses an injury nor
rules one out.

20
00:04:59,472 --> 00:05:33,408
For penetrating trauma, the card asks about firearm/type, range, angle
and ammunition if known, or an object size and whether a wound was a
slash or stab. Include wound number, possible entrances/exits, other
injuries, initial condition and consciousness. Scene safety comes
first. Do not delay treatment to recover a weapon, probe a wound or
invent a bullet trajectory; state uncertainty and avoid assuming the
visible wound count proves the number or path of injuries.

21
00:05:33,408 --> 00:05:49,464
Return to the seventy-four-year-old after the bathroom fall. Begin by
acknowledging that hospital feels frightening, then clarify whether
the patient is declining transport, an examination, a particular
procedure or all contact. These are different decisions.

22
00:05:49,464 --> 00:06:03,720
An original explanation is: The headache and repeated question after
the fall concern me. I cannot rule out an important injury here, and I
recommend further assessment. What is the hardest part of that plan
for you?

23
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:16,512
Allow the answer, then explain appropriate options without
guaranteeing that every test or destination can be chosen. Ask the
patient to describe the concern and what the proposed plan means for
them.

24
00:06:16,512 --> 00:06:29,280
Do not use one correct orientation answer as the conclusion. Keep
assessing injury and clinical change while supporting the
conversation; a fluent sentence does not erase the repeated-question
finding.

25
00:06:29,280 --> 00:06:47,856
For the first original update, reduce noise and provide the preferred
communication support. The patient then describes the head-injury
concern, explains how the recommendation relates to them and gives a
consistent reason for their choice. Record that evidence rather than
merely writing capacity confirmed.

26
00:06:47,856 --> 00:07:03,264
For the second update, the same question continues and the patient
cannot demonstrate understanding after appropriate support. That is an
unresolved assessment concern, not a reason to pretend an informed
refusal has been established.

27
00:07:03,264 --> 00:07:17,136
In either version, use the actual current legal and service pathway
and obtain indicated consultation. Do not turn the exercise into a
universal rule to force transport or to leave every patient who says
no.

28
00:07:17,136 --> 00:07:31,920
If responsiveness or another immediate clinical finding worsens,
return to the urgent assessment and explain the change. The previous
conversation is evidence about its time, not a permanent clearance for
the rest of the encounter.

29
00:07:31,920 --> 00:07:58,896
The Wednesday outline specifically connects refusal after a seizure
with family concern about a changed baseline. The fall case teaches a
transferable method, but it does not replace this preparation. In this
original rehearsal, an adult has stopped convulsing, pulls away from a
crowded doorway and says, I am fine; leave. The partner says the
person normally explains their own seizure plan clearly.

30
00:07:58,896 --> 00:08:25,872
Begin with a calm introduction and space when safe. An original
opening is: I can see you want room. May I explain why we are
concerned, and what assessment you would accept? Assign the partner a
specific clinical task. Consider ongoing recovery, oxygenation,
glucose and injury rather than assuming a psychiatric cause; immediate
breathing danger takes priority over finishing the conversation.

31
00:08:25,872 --> 00:08:57,360
Use the school four-domain prompts to hear the person explain the
concern, its relevance, options and choice; apply the distinct legal
standard already taught. Reveal an update: after communication
support, the person still cannot retain what was explained. Record the
actual responses and unresolved risk, reassess and consult through the
current pathway. Neither the word postictal nor a family request alone
authorizes force or establishes an informed refusal.

32
00:08:57,360 --> 00:09:34,704
Original documentation example: at thirteen twenty, a seated older
adult has a right-arm pressure of one hundred forty-six over eighty-
four and pulse seventy-six. At thirteen twenty-six, after moving to a
chair, the left-arm pressure is one hundred thirty-two over eighty and
pulse eighty-two. At thirteen thirty, after supported rest, the right-
arm pressure is one hundred forty over eighty-two and pulse seventy-
eight. Record all three separately, including the arm and movement.
The lower left-arm value should not replace the other observations.

33
00:09:34,704 --> 00:10:07,128
Worked interpretation: the arm and activity changed, so the three
pressures are not a clean same-condition sequence. Check symptoms,
perfusion and technique, then repeat under comparable conditions when
appropriate. Do not average away the switch or diagnose recovery from
the lowest number. If the person becomes newly confused or breathless,
act on that change instead of repeating measurements until one looks
reassuring. A precise record makes the uncertainty visible to the next
clinician.

34
00:10:07,128 --> 00:10:21,072
A worker in a poorly ventilated workshop becomes increasingly
restless, removes protective clothing and cannot answer a familiar
coworker. The temperature is high and a machine remains running.

35
00:10:21,072 --> 00:10:40,872
Treat the change as a possible medical emergency, not as a label. Keep
responders out of uncontrolled hazards, create space, use one calm
speaker, arrange appropriate support and assess airway, breathing,
temperature, glucose risk, injury and exposures as safely possible.

36
00:10:40,872 --> 00:11:09,408
Start with one connected chain rather than a list of brain parts. A
receptor detects a change, neural pathways transmit it, networks
integrate it with other information, and muscles or glands produce a
response. Central versus peripheral is an anatomical map; sensory,
integrative and motor are functional descriptions that cross that
boundary. A problem at several different points can change the same
visible behavior.

37
00:11:09,408 --> 00:11:37,872
Glia maintain the environment, myelin supports conduction, and
circulation and metabolism support the whole system. Therefore, a new
difficulty speaking, following a task or moving needs a physiological
assessment as well as respectful communication. It does not prove a
psychiatric cause, a damaged single brain region or lack of decision-
making capacity. Establish what changed from this person's baseline
and what could interfere with testing.

38
00:11:37,872 --> 00:12:09,600
The original diagram models a neuronal membrane, not a cardiac monitor
trace. The book uses minus seventy millivolts at rest, minus fifty-
five near threshold and plus thirty at the peak. These are example
values. Sufficient input triggers regenerative sodium entry; sodium-
channel inactivation and delayed potassium current then produce
repolarization and an afterhyperpolarization. The pump maintains
gradients rather than directly making every fast falling phase.

39
00:12:09,600 --> 00:12:39,000
In this model, stronger input generally changes firing frequency or
recruitment, not the height of each spike in direct proportion. Myelin
lets local current spread between nodes where spikes regenerate; a
single sodium ion does not travel from brain to toe. Explain the
mechanism without turning the example voltage into a patient threshold
or assuming every cell has the same waveform. A change of one hundred
millivolts is point one volt.

40
00:12:39,000 --> 00:13:10,704
At a chemical synapse, terminal calcium entry helps release
transmitter from vesicles. The transmitter crosses a short cleft and
acts through receptors on a target cell. Some receptors directly
control channels; others use intracellular signaling. The same
transmitter can have different effects at different receptor types.
Glutamate, GABA, acetylcholine, amines and peptides are not
interchangeable chemicals, and their names alone do not predict the
whole patient's behavior.

41
00:13:10,704 --> 00:13:43,080
Separate the switching and effector steps. A G protein uses GDP and
GTP cycling; the source caption that says ATP here is inconsistent
with its body. An effector such as adenylyl cyclase can separately use
ATP to make cyclic AMP. Drugs can affect release, receptors or
clearance, but a classroom mechanism does not identify a patient's
transmitter level or authorize a sedative. Connect exposure timing to
observed physiology and the actual clinical pathway.

42
00:13:43,080 --> 00:14:11,592
The assigned neurological chapter organizes examination into mental
status, cranial nerves, sensation, motor function and coordination,
with gait often listed separately. These are linked samples of
function. A problem understanding a command can affect a movement test
even if the muscle can work; pain or poor vision can also distort a
result. Check the task, baseline and conditions rather than diagnosing
from a single failed item.

43
00:14:11,592 --> 00:14:42,168
The neurological mental-status examination overlaps with, but is not
identical to, the psychiatric mental-state examination. Neither is the
legal capacity test. New focal findings, reduced responsiveness or an
acute change still need urgent medical assessment. A rapid screen
directs the next action; completing every classroom test must not
delay support or time-sensitive conveyance. State what was safely
examined and what remains uncertain.

44
00:14:42,168 --> 00:15:12,216
Before calling a response abnormal, make the question accessible.
Reduce noise, check hearing and language needs, and explain one
appropriate task. Then distinguish language comprehension or
production from motor articulation. Someone can speak fluently without
understanding, or understand while struggling to produce words. A
familiar accent, a word-list score or a nod is not enough to settle
either neurological function or consent.

45
00:15:12,216 --> 00:15:40,896
If new naming difficulty or an asymmetric movement persists after
communication support, do not explain it away as stress. Establish
timing and baseline, compare the other domains and assess immediate
threats. The reading's classical language areas help explain networks,
but do not give a precise lesion diagnosis from one answer. Ask
whether the finding changes urgency, what support is needed and what
the receiving clinician must know.

46
00:15:40,896 --> 00:16:11,616
Keep the functions separate. Detecting a peripheral object while
holding fixation tests a field; following a target tests eye movement.
Pupillary light response involves optic input, bilateral central
connections and oculomotor parasympathetic output. Compare the
observation with drugs, prior eye problems and other findings. The
textbook's optic nerve versus tract naming does not make the chiasm a
peripheral-to-central border; the optic nerve is CNS-derived too.

47
00:16:11,616 --> 00:16:43,848
Source corrections also matter: the sixth-nerve nucleus is pontine,
and an MLF-related internuclear pattern primarily impairs adduction,
not simply abduction. In the field, report the actual movement and
associated deficits rather than pretending the cause is certain. Do
not force head rotation, neck resistance or a gag response in an
unsafe context. A normal-looking single pupil or gaze observation does
not rule out every serious neurological cause.

48
00:16:43,848 --> 00:17:13,392
Compare meaningful sensory and motor findings across sides, using the
actual trained method and safe equipment. An arm that pronates and
drifts is an abnormal observation, not the reassuring negative result
mistakenly described in one source sentence. Tone, reflexes and
strength form patterns with important timing and injury limits. Do not
diagnose a cord level from one point or use an improvised sharp
implement to complete a chart.

49
00:17:13,392 --> 00:17:44,736
Coordination depends on sensory feedback, vision, motor output and
cerebellar connections. Ataxia can have central, peripheral, toxic or
metabolic explanations; it does not prove intoxication. If the person
is already unstable or injured, observe safely and document why a gait
task was not attempted. Do not demand tandem walking, hopping or a
push to prove abnormality, and do not mistake a normal single task for
a complete exclusion of stroke.

50
00:17:44,736 --> 00:18:14,280
In this original case, a fifty-four-year-old mechanic is described as
stressed after an argument. You reduce workshop noise and clarify
hearing and language needs. New naming difficulty and right-arm drift
remain. Family report a normal baseline at twelve ten, and the
simulation gives a measured glucose of six point two millimoles per
litre. Those findings are not explained away by emotion or by one
glucose in a usual range.

51
00:18:14,280 --> 00:18:48,576
Assess and support immediate threats, record last-known-well and
serial findings, and escalate through the applicable stroke pathway.
Symptom improvement does not make the episode safe to ignore or prove
there was no infarction. Do not import the textbook story's aspirin as
an undifferentiated field order. Handover what you measured, what
changed after communication support, the times, actions and remaining
uncertainty. Neurological findings inform the actual decision; they do
not replace the separate consent process.

52
00:18:48,576 --> 00:19:16,200
In this original cafe rehearsal, a forty-six-year-old repeatedly
stands and says the legs cannot stay still after a medication
increase. Ask what changed, obtain the actual product and last dose,
and examine glucose, vital signs and movement. Akathisia is one
possible explanation, not proof of defiance or a final diagnosis. Keep
illness, substances and other adverse effects in view.

53
00:19:16,200 --> 00:19:46,392
Now change one feature: fever, marked rigidity and impaired
responsiveness appear. That pattern raises concern for a dangerous
drug reaction such as neuroleptic malignant syndrome, among other
causes. Reassess airway, ventilation and circulation, obtain
temperature and timing, and escalate urgently. Do not keep treating a
behavioral label while physiology worsens, or convert the textbook
specialist-drug examples into a PCP treatment order.

54
00:19:46,392 --> 00:20:17,640
In the next variation, recent diarrhea, lithium use and new
unsteadiness replace the original medication history. Lithium has a
narrow therapeutic margin and renal elimination. Fluid loss, kidney
impairment or an interacting medicine can change exposure even when
the prescribed dose was followed. Ask about intake, vomiting, urine
changes, last dose and medicines such as NSAIDs or diuretics; do not
simply call the presentation recurrent mania.

55
00:20:17,640 --> 00:20:47,808
Assess the actual patient and support immediate threats while
obtaining appropriate clinical help. A prescribed milligram dose is
not a serum concentration, and the textbook toxicity bands are not
guaranteed symptom stages. Do not wait for laboratory confirmation
before responding to deterioration, force water into an unsafe
swallow, or calculate a catch-up dose from a maintenance table.
Handover the measured findings, exposure timing and uncertainty.

56
00:20:47,808 --> 00:21:19,296
After a sedative exposure, the patient becomes quiet but breathing is
slower and shallower. That is not automatically successful de-
escalation. Check airway patency, effective ventilation, circulation
and responsiveness, compare the trend and provide indicated support.
Alcohol, opioids and other depressants can compound the problem. RASS
helps describe arousal; it cannot replace respiratory assessment or
determine capacity by itself.

57
00:21:19,296 --> 00:21:51,048
The reading names flumazenil, but its availability does not replace
airway and ventilation support. Reversal can provoke seizures in high-
risk circumstances and sedation may recur. Obtain appropriate clinical
or toxicology help rather than treating every drowsy person with an
assumed antidote. Current G01 places pharmacological restraint under
ACP interventions; a nursing dose table or a student lecture does not
create PCP administration or reversal authority.

58
00:21:51,048 --> 00:22:20,784
Use this original cook case to connect all three assigned CNS ranges.
The forty-seven-year-old has a four-month history of back pain, new
abdominal discomfort and tremor after reducing alcohol. A companion
answers many questions. These findings need a safe, accessible
assessment, not an immediate psychiatric, withdrawal or abuse label.
Establish the person's own account, baseline, timing and immediate
physiological needs.

59
00:22:20,784 --> 00:22:48,216
Set a useful communication goal, make the message understandable,
listen, clarify and check the response. Do not treat politeness, a nod
or a quiet manner as proof of understanding or comfort. Support the
person without promising secrecy or a service you cannot guarantee.
The whole reading is communication, pain and psychosocial assessment;
its Chapter Twenty is not an end-of-life chapter.

60
00:22:48,216 --> 00:23:15,744
Distinguish pain from nociception. Transduction begins at sensory
endings, transmission carries and processes neural activity,
perception is the experienced pain, and modulation changes processing
through spinal and descending influences. The source's gate model is
not a literal valve, and the brain is not consciously choosing a
person's pain. An inability to speak does not mean the person cannot
experience pain.

61
00:23:15,744 --> 00:23:43,992
In the cook case, calm behavior and an ordinary-looking back do not
invalidate the pain report. Biological, psychological and social
influences can change the experience without making it imaginary. Ask
about location, quality, onset, function and associated symptoms. Do
not infer severity from apparent injury size or replace assessment
with a promise that a positive attitude or rubbing the site will
remove the pain.

62
00:23:43,992 --> 00:24:13,440
The source repeatedly uses six months for chronic pain; current IASP
classification uses persistence or recurrence beyond three months.
That difference does not make the cook's new abdominal pain part of an
established harmless problem. Duration, site, mechanism and intensity
are separate axes. Referred pain is felt away from its source;
radiation describes spread. Neither a location nor a descriptive word
confirms the cause.

63
00:24:13,440 --> 00:24:42,456
A transient breakthrough flare is different from continuously
uncontrolled background pain or a new illness. Neuropathic pain
involves a lesion or disease of the somatosensory system; tingling
alone does not prove that diagnosis. Diabetic neuropathy is not
explained only by reduced circulation. Use these mechanisms to ask
better questions and communicate uncertainty, not to select a new drug
or invent a rescue dose.

64
00:24:42,456 --> 00:25:10,536
Use a pain tool the person can understand. A number records their
reported intensity, not injury severity or an automatic medication
dose. Wong-Baker FACES is also self-report: the person selects the
face representing their pain. It is not a way to match their
expression, measure mood or score an unresponsive person. Use the
actual appropriate instructions and do not copy or modify licensed
artwork.

65
00:25:10,536 --> 00:25:40,416
For an appropriate observational assessment, FLACC has five categories
scored zero to two each, so the maximum is ten, not the textbook's
twelve. In this original addition exercise, supplied scores of one,
zero, one, two and one total five out of ten. The exercise checks
arithmetic only. Actual scoring needs the defined criteria, a suitable
patient and context; a number does not identify the cause of distress.

66
00:25:40,416 --> 00:26:08,952
The source gives sympathetic and parasympathetic changes as pain and
relief clues. They are not a pain meter. A normal pulse cannot refute
pain; reduced heart rate, small pupils or quietness cannot establish
comfortable recovery. With the cook, compare the actual report and
function with breathing, responsiveness and other findings. An
improved pain score with reduced ventilation is not an uncomplicated
success.

67
00:26:08,952 --> 00:26:37,656
Record the tool, time frame, symptoms, goal, intervention and
response. Reassess at a time appropriate to the actual route and
expected effect, and immediately if deterioration appears. Onset is
not the same as duration of action. The assigned opioid table mixes
product strengths, patch rates and weight-based quantities; it is not
one universal adult dose or permission to repeat a medicine until the
number falls.

68
00:26:37,656 --> 00:27:08,448
Describe mood, affect, speech, motor activity, thought form and
content, perception, attention and memory. Mood is more sustained;
affect is the observed expression, not a lie detector. Consider
baseline, communication, pain, medicines and medical causes. A nod,
one correct gesture or orientation to four items is not a legal
capacity test or proof that a new cognitive change has no serious
cause.

69
00:27:08,448 --> 00:27:41,256
Ask neutrally about frightening perceptions and actual safety concerns
rather than arguing or endorsing an unverified event. Thoughts,
intent, plans, access and past behavior are separate parts of a risk
assessment. Do not require all of them to be present before taking
concern seriously or let protective factors erase it. Use the trained
safety, supervision, escalation and accepted-handover process, not an
unfamiliar questionnaire as an independent clearance test.

70
00:27:41,256 --> 00:28:10,848
The cook's tremor after reducing alcohol raises a concern; it does not
establish the cause of every symptom. Ask about timing, amount,
previous seizures or severe withdrawal, medicines, co-use and other
illness. Withdrawal windows overlap and vary. A positive alcohol level
neither rules it in nor rules it out, and CIWA-Ar scores can reflect
other conditions. Do not wait for a textbook clock before responding
to a severe finding.

71
00:28:10,848 --> 00:28:43,080
Alcohol and sedative withdrawal can be dangerous, while opioid
intoxication may threaten ventilation. Substance-use diagnosis,
intoxication and physiological dependence are not interchangeable.
Screeners need their actual instructions: the source confuses the
single alcohol screening question, SASQ, with the longer dependence
questionnaire, SADQ. The nursing drug and withdrawal tables are not
medication orders or a reason to disregard the patient's own account.

72
00:28:43,080 --> 00:29:09,264
In a safely private conversation, the cook reports that a partner has
threatened them and controls access to medicines. Acknowledge the
account and ask about current needs and safety without demanding the
whole story. A companion answering earlier was a cue to explore, not
proof. Do not confront the partner, announce the disclosure in their
presence or use them to interpret a sensitive conversation.

73
00:29:09,264 --> 00:29:39,744
Consent, medical care, evidence collection and police involvement are
different questions. A relationship never creates entitlement to sex,
and refusing sex is not itself abuse. Ask what contact or material can
be received safely; a shared phone or printed note may be discovered.
Follow the actual clinical and safeguarding process while respecting
the person's involvement, and document the words, findings,
consultation and accepted next step.

74
00:29:39,744 --> 00:30:06,312
Keep the source's American reporting examples separate from BC
practice. A child is under nineteen under the BC child-protection Act,
and the actual reporting provision includes a specific Indigenous-
authority route. Do not import a twelve-hour rule or treat poverty
alone as proof. Preserve the relevant words and safety concern; a
clinician should not conduct a repeated leading interview to
manufacture evidence.

75
00:30:06,312 --> 00:30:33,720
Adult safeguarding uses a different framework and may involve a
designated agency under specified circumstances. The chapter's broad
mandatory-report claim is not a universal BC rule for every older or
sexually assaulted adult. Apply the relevant law, service process and
consultation to the actual case, rather than guessing authority from
age. Immediate threats still require the appropriate safety and
clinical response.

76
00:30:33,720 --> 00:31:00,600
Handover the cook's new symptoms and actual findings, the duration and
pattern of pain, recent alcohol reduction and other exposures, and the
response to authorized support. Keep the patient's report separate
from the companion's account and your hypotheses. A calm appearance
does not invalidate pain, a tremor does not settle withdrawal and a
disclosure does not remove the need to assess the acute medical
problem.

77
00:31:00,600 --> 00:31:26,736
Confirm who has accepted the next step and communicate privately
through the appropriate secure route. Record the person's choices,
what remains uncertain and what will happen if the plan fails.
Supporting immediate safety is not a promise to solve every social
problem. Rehearse again by changing only one fact, such as reduced
responsiveness, then explain which priority and clinical pathway must
change.

78
00:31:26,736 --> 00:31:42,408
The hot workshop combines a changing patient with machinery and a
narrow exit. Do not enter an uncontrolled exposure to begin the
interview. State the hazard and obtain the appropriate control or
assistance before assuming contact is safe.

79
00:31:42,408 --> 00:31:58,248
Assign one lead communicator. Ask the partner for a clear observation
or clinical task within the safe plan and request a report. A vague
instruction to watch everything does not establish who owns airway
observation or exit awareness.

80
00:31:58,248 --> 00:32:14,112
Use short, non-threatening communication while assessing the possible
medical change. Heat, exposure, injury, glucose disturbance and other
causes remain possibilities; do not diagnose from a historical
excited-delirium label.

81
00:32:14,112 --> 00:32:25,368
If specific conduct or the environment becomes unsafe, coordinate
withdrawal and support. The goal is safe care, not proving that
conversation can solve every scene.

82
00:32:25,368 --> 00:32:51,384
The actual third PMHN range is schizophrenia, not mood disorders.
Together the four assigned ranges teach anger and safety, rights,
psychotic symptoms, personality patterns and ongoing care. Their
content does not itself supply a complete field protocol for the
school's historical agitation label. Use the actual school monitoring
tasks and current service pathway, while preserving this broader
reading.

83
00:32:51,384 --> 00:33:22,080
In this original case, a thirty-two-year-old museum guide has an
established schizophrenia diagnosis and a recent prescription change.
Today they are pacing, distressed and experiencing jaw pulling. The
diagnosis does not establish the cause. Assess current physiology,
breathing, responsiveness, exposure timing and other findings, with
appropriate urgent help. Do not assume that restlessness means more
antipsychotic medication is needed.

84
00:33:22,080 --> 00:33:51,672
Positive symptoms are abnormally present experiences, not good
symptoms. Negative symptoms describe reduced expected function, not
bad character. Cognitive difficulties concern attention, memory and
using information. Describe what the guide actually says or does and
compare with baseline. A quiet expression does not prove comfort, and
a new reduction in movement or speech is not automatically a chronic
negative symptom.

85
00:33:51,672 --> 00:34:23,712
If a person reports a threatening voice, ask neutrally about the
experience, its effect, any command and current safety. Do not argue,
ridicule or confirm an unverified event. A command does not guarantee
obedience, but a reassuring answer does not complete risk assessment.
Psychosis, delirium, a substance effect and schizophrenia are not
interchangeable. A normal test or positive toxicology result does not
exclude every medical explanation.

86
00:34:23,712 --> 00:34:55,344
Akathisia can produce distressing restlessness and pacing. Acute
dystonia can produce sustained involuntary contractions, while drug-
induced parkinsonism and tardive dyskinesia have different patterns.
The museum guide's jaw and movement findings need assessment, not a
diagnosis by resemblance. Verify the actual drug, formulation, dose
history and timing. Do not copy the nursing table's dose changes or
treatment choices into an unauthorized field order.

87
00:34:55,344 --> 00:35:27,744
Relevant medication exposure with hyperthermia, rigidity, altered
mental status or autonomic instability raises an emergency concern. Do
not wait for exactly two weeks or every classic feature before
escalating. Sedation, orthostatic symptoms, anticholinergic effects
and other medicines also matter. After authorized care, follow
breathing, responsiveness and the actual response; a quieter person
does not prove that either the illness or the treatment risk has
resolved.

88
00:35:27,744 --> 00:36:00,144
The source repeatedly treats written consent as the whole process. In
BC, consent can be oral, written or inferred from conduct, specific to
the care agreed to. Explain the concern, proposed care, relevant
risks, benefits and alternatives, and support questions and
understanding. Do not infer incapability from schizophrenia,
disagreement, distress or one orientation result. The existing school
refusal conversation remains an aid, not a statutory score.

89
00:36:00,144 --> 00:36:30,504
An emergency, a substitute decision and a Mental Health Act status
require their actual conditions and roles. The source's generic
seventy-two-hour hold is not the BC process. Neither a medication
order nor a relative's insistence gives unlimited authority. In the
guide case, establish what is proposed, the patient's actual decision
and the lawful next step, while addressing immediate medical danger
and obtaining appropriate consultation.

90
00:36:30,504 --> 00:37:03,192
The restraint table mixes renewal, evaluation, monitoring, care and
removal. Its American four, two and one-hour figures describe age-
based order-renewal limits for violent or self-destructive restraint;
the initial face-to-face evaluation is a separate within-one-hour
requirement. This corrects the textbook, not a BC field schedule. Do
not wait for any maximum time before reassessing, and do not use a
renewal limit as permission to continue a harmful intervention.

91
00:37:03,192 --> 00:37:35,976
Seclusion is not simply offering a quiet room that someone can leave.
Restriction needs the actual lawful, trained and least-restrictive
process, with appropriate assessment, monitoring and review. Do not
adopt the source table's prone-position suggestion. The existing
school hard stops remain in place. Document behavior and risk,
alternatives, authority, intervention and response, and communicate a
deterioration immediately rather than reporting only that the person
has become calm.

92
00:37:35,976 --> 00:38:08,976
The chapter opening names only one example per cluster, but the later
pages describe all ten patterns. Cluster A contains paranoid, schizoid
and schizotypal; B contains antisocial, borderline, histrionic and
narcissistic; C contains avoidant, dependent and obsessive-compulsive
personality patterns. Learn their differences in the paired reading.
They are longitudinal descriptions, not a way to label a frightened
person from one disagreement or decide who deserves care.

93
00:38:08,976 --> 00:38:39,648
Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder is not OCD, and schizoid or
schizotypal is not schizophrenia. Conduct disorder does not
automatically convert to antisocial personality disorder at eighteen.
A diagnosis also does not determine intent, capacity or medical
stability. Respect cultural and developmental context, explore
functioning and actual support needs, and keep an acute medical change
separate from an established personality or psychiatric history.

94
00:38:39,648 --> 00:39:09,864
The personality chapter discusses CBT, DBT, practical supports and a
collaborative crisis plan. DBT skills include mindfulness, distress
tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness; one
brief exercise is not the full treatment. Ask what the person already
finds helpful and whether it is safe now. Neither an ice technique, a
breathing count nor a diagnostic label guarantees relief or replaces
urgent assessment.

95
00:39:09,864 --> 00:39:41,016
Treat self-harm and suicidal concerns as current needs, even if the
person has attended before or a chart uses an attention-seeking label.
Assess injury, intent, distress and safety without punishment. Do not
require a written account before providing necessary care or engaging
with the person. Predictable boundaries can coexist with compassion. A
promise not to self-harm is not proof of safety or a substitute for an
accepted support and supervision plan.

96
00:39:41,016 --> 00:40:09,912
For the museum guide, hand over the new pacing and jaw findings,
medication-change timeline, current examination, the person's own
report and the response to authorized support. Distinguish an
observation, a hypothesis and a confirmed history. Protect privacy and
use appropriate chosen support. Do not let a psychiatric label erase a
new medical concern, or let a social or safety concern cancel the
clinical assessment.

97
00:40:09,912 --> 00:40:41,568
Agree a realistic next step and confirm who has accepted it. A useful
plan includes what matters to the person, what remains uncertain and
what will happen if distress or physiology worsens. Afterwards, review
communication, environmental triggers, access barriers and clinician
assumptions without blame or identifying informal disclosures. This
closes the reading-to-practice loop while leaving formal diagnosis,
prescribing and supervised skill assessment with their proper
services.

98
00:40:41,568 --> 00:40:57,408
Prone positioning and pressure on the back, chest or neck can impair
ventilation. If a patient is restricted by any means, maintain access
to the face and breathing, keep the position as safe as possible and
summon authorized help.

99
00:40:57,408 --> 00:41:15,840
The saved school outline specifies q5min vital signs and respiratory
monitoring as a station memory target. It is not an all-purpose safe
monitoring interval. Follow current protocol, maintain observation
appropriate to the risk and escalate any deterioration immediately.

100
00:41:15,840 --> 00:41:46,176
The second school preparation case begins with police-applied prone
restraint, not just a noisy scene. For an original transfer round, an
adult outside a venue is hot and struggling while lying face down; a
bystander reports pressure over the upper back. Explain the immediate
ventilation concern to the responsible officers and coordinate prompt
correction of the dangerous position and pressure with trained
personnel, while maintaining scene safety and airway access.

101
00:41:46,176 --> 00:42:13,872
Keep the source distinction explicit. The school calls for an
immediate stop to prone or hog-tied danger. The current public G01
says three-quarter-prone if possible when law enforcement has applied
restraint. That is not permission for fully face-down compression, and
neither wording is a hands-on release technique. In class, rehearse
the verbal coordination; have the instructor demonstrate any
authorized physical skill.

102
00:42:13,872 --> 00:42:41,856
Now reveal that the struggling suddenly stops and responses weaken.
Interrupt the interview and reassess airway, actual ventilation and
circulation immediately; arrange the required support. Record the
change and timing. The school five-minute vital-sign target does not
authorize waiting. Heat, apparent unusual strength or reduced reaction
to pain do not establish a single diagnosis or permit extra force.

103
00:42:41,856 --> 00:42:56,712
For an original update, the worker stops speaking and becomes less
responsive. Pause the interview and assess the new clinical threat as
safely possible. A quiet patient is not automatically an improved
patient.

104
00:42:56,712 --> 00:43:12,384
If the person is restricted by another agency, communicate the
immediate airway and breathing concern, maintain safe access and
obtain appropriate assistance. Do not apply an untrained release or
restraint technique as a classroom improvisation.

105
00:43:12,384 --> 00:43:26,424
The school five-minute vital-sign target remains a recall item, not a
wait instruction. Check a change when it occurs, and continue the
required physiological monitoring rather than using a behavioural
score as a substitute.

106
00:43:26,424 --> 00:43:40,896
Tell the receiving clinician what changed, when, and what happened
around the change. Distinguish medication known to have been
administered from an assumption based on drowsiness. Document the
actual response and unresolved risk.

107
00:43:40,896 --> 00:43:54,720
Chemical sedation is not a routine PCP action and this lesson supplies
no drug dose or administration sequence. The current BCEHS G01 pathway
distinguishes interventions by provider level.

108
00:43:54,720 --> 00:44:08,736
A calm appearance after medication is not proof of stability. A
trained authorized provider must monitor for respiratory depression,
airway compromise, hypotension and worsening illness according to
protocol.

109
00:44:08,736 --> 00:44:42,000
G03 makes interfacility transfer a planned clinical encounter. Confirm
identity, obtain a meaningful sending-team handover and supporting
documents, compare the current assessment with the pre-transport risk
assessment, and review the individualized transport plan. Check staff,
equipment, escort and family arrangements against actual risk and
space. A patient who received medication before departure needs
assessment of its effect, not an assumption that the paperwork
guarantees stability.

110
00:44:42,000 --> 00:45:15,552
The current G03 PCP branch permits medication during transport only as
maintenance of therapy described in an individualized transport plan
that fits PCP scope; CliniCall must confirm that plan. This is not a
blanket ban on every medication associated with sedation, but neither
is it permission to start independent field chemical restraint. If the
patient, safety or transport needs no longer match the plan, seek re-
conference and the appropriate resource rather than stretching the
original authorization.

111
00:45:15,552 --> 00:45:25,824
A person with advanced illness asks for help with new severe
breathlessness at home. A partner says the patient has a no-CPR form
and should not be transported.

112
00:45:25,824 --> 00:45:43,872
Assess the acute change and the patient’s wishes directly. Verify
applicable documentation using the current BCEHS pathway. A no-CPR
instruction does not mean no assessment, symptom relief or other care,
and it does not independently decide transport.

113
00:45:43,872 --> 00:46:17,808
Delirium is an acute, fluctuating change in attention and awareness
from the individual baseline. Hyperactive, hypoactive and mixed forms
explain why both agitation and unusual withdrawal can matter. Sleep-
cycle, cognition or perception may also change. Do not label a newly
quiet, sleepy person comfortable without assessment. P02 says delirium
near the end of life still deserves recognition and relief, rather
than being dismissed as a normal inevitability.

114
00:46:17,808 --> 00:46:53,160
Look for contributors that match the person and goals: infection,
hypoxia, organ failure, metabolic disturbance, dehydration,
withdrawal, poorly controlled pain, sleep loss, constipation, urinary
retention and an unfamiliar environment. Reduce avoidable stimulation
and provide reassuring familiar contact when helpful. Restraint can
worsen delirium. A behavior score describes arousal but does not
determine the cause or replace breathing, perfusion and a focused
assessment.

115
00:46:53,160 --> 00:47:23,256
Grief is a personal response to loss; mourning is its outward, social
or ritual expression; bereavement refers to having experienced a
death-related loss. These terms overlap without being identical. A
person may grieve a lost role, health, independence, relationship or
expected future before anyone dies. A quiet expression does not
establish absence of grief, and visible tears do not determine its
severity.

116
00:47:23,256 --> 00:47:55,968
The five familiar labels are denial, anger, bargaining, depression and
acceptance. The actual source figure shows both continuum and random
paths, and the narrative allows repetition and skipped labels.
Contemporary grief guidance does not establish one mandatory stage
sequence or deadline. The word depression in this model is not
automatically a diagnosis of major depressive disorder. Acceptance
need not mean approval, forgetting or an absence of distress.

117
00:47:55,968 --> 00:48:28,752
The chapter’s chronic, delayed, exaggerated and masked categories
describe patterns sometimes called complicated or dysfunctional grief.
They are not field diagnoses or a universal clock. Ask about
persistent suffering, daily function, supports and danger; qualified
assessment must distinguish grief, depression, trauma and other
illness. The named rating tools, including grief inventories and
depression scales, are not interchangeable PCP diagnostic instruments.

118
00:48:28,752 --> 00:48:43,416
Return to the person with advanced illness and new breathlessness. Ask
directly what help they want now. Staying with a partner may be an
important goal, but it does not remove the need to assess the change
and available relief.

119
00:48:43,416 --> 00:48:58,392
Ask permission to review the relevant care document and clarify what
it actually directs. The presence of a MOST form is not itself a
particular resuscitation instruction; read the applicable order
instead of accepting a label.

120
00:48:58,392 --> 00:49:15,624
Use a comfort-focused approach while assessment and coordination
continue. When appropriate, simple positioning, a calmer environment
and airflow may help; use current symptom guidance and the patient’s
response, not a fixed comfort checklist for everyone.

121
00:49:15,624 --> 00:49:26,592
Contact the relevant care team or consultation route with the
patient’s goals and clinical findings. Do not promise home management
before an actual workable plan is established.

122
00:49:26,592 --> 00:49:55,320
Apply the assigned ethics chapter to the palliative station. Autonomy
asks whose informed decision is being made. Beneficence and
nonmaleficence require comparison of benefit and harm in this person's
circumstances. Justice asks whether barriers have prevented fair
participation, and fidelity requires honest commitments. Naming all
five principles is not a justified plan unless you explain how they
change the available actions.

123
00:49:55,320 --> 00:50:24,000
Recognize the clinical and ethical cues, identify the actual conflict,
compare feasible alternatives and obtain the appropriate decision
before acting. Then evaluate the response. Do not obey an unclear
instruction blindly, invent a treatment outside your role or postpone
immediate support while filling an ethics table. Record the reasoning,
consultation, action and reassessment trigger so another clinician can
follow the decision.

124
00:50:24,000 --> 00:50:54,072
In this original variation, a sixty-eight-year-old artist with serious
lung disease asks to speak privately and receive relief before
deciding about travel. A sibling requests every detail and produces a
financial power of attorney. Assess immediate needs while giving the
person an accessible opportunity to explain what matters. Family worry
deserves support, but does not automatically displace a capable
patient or permit unrestricted disclosure.

125
00:50:54,072 --> 00:51:25,080
The American durable-power-of-attorney description is not the BC rule.
A BC enduring power of attorney concerns financial and legal affairs,
not health-care consent merely because the person named is present.
Verify the actual document and any relevant health-care authority
through the applicable process. Keep the present patient decision,
permission to share information and substitute authority as separate
questions; do not settle all three from a family title.

126
00:51:25,080 --> 00:51:54,456
The assigned source sometimes restricts palliation to failed curative
treatment. Keep the broader correction visible: palliative care can be
integrated early during serious illness and addresses more than
physical pain. An expected change is not a reliable countdown, and
quietness is not proof of comfort. Assess what the artist reports, the
breathing pattern and the actual response to authorized support rather
than assuming all symptoms belong to dying.

127
00:51:54,456 --> 00:52:19,056
Explain the relevant options without using your personal beliefs to
steer the answer. Reduce information overload, address hearing and
language needs and ask the person to explain the plan in their own
words. Teach-back checks your explanation, not whether the patient
deserves a choice. Agree on what can be shared with the sibling, what
they can safely help with and what must be passed to the responsible
care team.

128
00:52:19,056 --> 00:52:47,280
Before describing a plan as complete, check whether the actual team
accepted follow-up and whether the chosen support person is willing
and able to carry out the agreed role. A voicemail is not an accepted
handover, and an unavailable hospice team is not a universal
instruction never to seek emergency help. Document the artist's own
decision, verified authority, observed response, unresolved concern
and the feasible fallback.

129
00:52:47,280 --> 00:53:16,200
Support realistic hope and the person's preferred cultural or
spiritual practices without prescribing a grief stage or copying a
religion table onto an individual. After the encounter, reflect on the
strain, seek appropriate support and protect confidentiality. Ethical
consultation and colleague support help you reason; they do not
transfer responsibility, replace urgent care or require the patient to
accept your own beliefs about a good death.

130
00:53:16,200 --> 00:53:53,064
Group the nitrous exclusions by mechanism. Gas-filled spaces can
expand: pneumothorax, air embolism, decompression illness and gross
abdominal distension matter. Current guidance also lists bullous
emphysema. Inability to cooperate or altered awareness defeats safe
self-administration; inhalation injury and recent nitroglycerin within
five minutes are additional listed exclusions. The printed card puts
abdominal distension among cautions, while the current monograph lists
gross distension as a contraindication. Preserve that distinction.

131
00:53:53,064 --> 00:54:23,520
Self-administered is a safety condition, not a command to press the
interface onto a drowsy patient. Explain the method and expected
sensations, observe pain and function alongside awareness, breathing,
perfusion and adverse effects, and record what actually happened. Keep
environmental ventilation and patient ventilation separate: an exhaust
fan reduces occupational exposure, but does not ventilate the lungs of
an inadequately breathing patient.

132
00:54:23,520 --> 00:54:57,552
The printed CHECK list is internally inconsistent: it omits the
established malignant-hyperthermia item in the mnemonic list and ends
with adult dosages. British Columbia Emergency Health Services expands
CHECK as cardiac instability or respiratory depression,
hypersensitivity, established malignant hyperthermia, consciousness
and kidneys. This does not replace the separate full contraindication
list. A neat mnemonic is useful only if it preserves the actual
exclusion, not if it makes a missing risk disappear.

133
00:54:57,552 --> 00:55:31,968
Keep three ceilings distinct. The source and current adult pathway
give three milliliters, with a repeat after twenty minutes and a six-
milliliter total; current guidance further limits a patient to six
milliliters in twenty-four hours and fifteen over a week. Provider
exposure is a different limit: three doses, nine milliliters total in
twenty-four hours. Do not give a patient nine because that is the
responder exposure limit, or treat a weekly maximum as an amount to
finish. Record earlier doses and the time.

134
00:55:31,968 --> 00:56:10,464
Breakthrough pain is a temporary worsening on a background of
otherwise reasonably controlled pain. A prescribed rescue dose
supplements rather than automatically replaces the regular schedule.
Keep the exact medicine, route, formulation, regular twenty-four-hour
amount and every rescue administration separate. The source total
daily dose includes regular plus breakthrough use; that is not
necessarily the same denominator as total regular daily dose. Repeated
rescue use calls for reassessment and an authorized plan review, not
endless unsupervised escalation.

135
00:56:10,464 --> 00:56:51,000
The P03 text itself changes the breakthrough denominator: one passage
uses ten percent of the regular daily opioid amount, while the
titration passage says ten percent of the new regular dose after
dividing the daily total by six. These are not equivalent. For an
original arithmetic illustration, ten percent of one hundred twenty is
twelve, but ten percent of one hundred twenty divided by six is two.
This exposes a sixfold difference. This illustration is not a patient
prescription. Verify the actual prescribed regimen and obtain
clarification rather than silently choosing one interpretation.

136
00:56:51,000 --> 00:57:02,136
The partner now says the document is not available and only remembers
that the patient did not want CPR. Record this as a reported
preference, not as a form you have inspected.

137
00:57:02,136 --> 00:57:19,200
P07 requires CliniCall guidance when clear resuscitation documentation
is unavailable, including a verbal order claim. Do not invent an
arrest decision from the palliative label or a remembered document;
communicate the actual uncertainty promptly.

138
00:57:19,200 --> 00:57:33,576
In this breathing patient, continue appropriate assessment and symptom
care while clarification occurs. A no-CPR instruction concerns
resuscitation, not a general instruction to stop every helpful action.

139
00:57:33,576 --> 00:57:49,680
If the clinical situation changes, use the current resuscitation,
goals-of-care and consultation pathways for that situation. Do not
turn this classroom comparison into independent authority to pronounce
death or withhold an unreviewed intervention.

140
00:57:49,680 --> 00:58:27,264
Dyspnea is the person’s uncomfortable breathing sensation; it may
occur without hypoxemia. Assess the actual effort, speech,
responsiveness, circulation, saturation/baseline and possible cause
while clarifying relief goals. Appropriate positioning, reassurance
and gentle airflow may help. The P04 five-minute airflow trial applies
to its nonhypoxic context, not an instruction to wait through
inadequate ventilation, collapse or another immediate threat. Reassess
the patient’s reported relief and objective state separately.

141
00:58:27,264 --> 00:59:04,152
Noisy secretions can upset a family without proving the patient is
distressed. Assess the person: facial tension, effort, responsiveness,
airway and goals matter more than sound alone. P06 favors suitable
side-lying/head elevation and allows gentle anterior suction for
accessible oral secretions, not routine deep suction to silence every
rattle. Oxygen for actual hypoxia is a different question from oxygen
to remove a sound. Explain what you see and what a comfort
intervention is intended to change.

142
00:59:04,152 --> 00:59:37,008
Table 36.4 organizes possible dying-related changes by system. Reduced
perfusion may appear as cool or mottled skin and a changing pulse or
pressure; breathing can become irregular, effortful or noisy as cough
and secretion clearance change. These findings are not a fixed early-
middle-late timetable, nor a substitute for assessing a new treatable
problem. Abnormal or agonal breathing requires the real emergency and
goals-of-care decision, not a stage label.

143
00:59:37,008 --> 01:00:09,240
Reduced intake can be upsetting to caregivers who associate food with
care. Explain the observed swallowing and comfort needs without
promising that fluids will reverse dying. Offer mouth and lip care
and, where desired and safe, assistance with suitable sips or food. Do
not force intake into someone who cannot swallow safely. Clinically
assisted hydration requires individual assessment of possible benefit
and harm; neither always give fluids nor never give fluids is the
lesson.

144
01:00:09,240 --> 01:00:23,568
Palliative guidance supports care at home when it fits the person’s
wishes and the actual situation. It does not require transport for
every symptom, and it does not guarantee that every new crisis can be
managed at home.

145
01:00:23,568 --> 01:00:38,904
Review the observed symptom response, remaining distress and the
accepted follow-up plan. If symptoms remain uncontrolled or a safe
arrangement is unavailable, explain the available options and obtain
the indicated clinical decision.

146
01:00:38,904 --> 01:00:52,584
Check whether the chosen support person can and wants to perform the
agreed role. Family presence is not automatic evidence of available
care, and a phone message is not confirmation that the home team will
attend.

147
01:00:52,584 --> 01:01:04,848
Use teach-back for the practical next step and contingency, then hand
over responsibility clearly. Record what is agreed, what remains
uncertain and who will respond if the plan fails.

148
01:01:04,848 --> 01:01:41,208
For authorized teaching or simulation, explain the purpose and
contact, agree on consent/privacy and the patient preferred
participation, and offer supported self-administration when
appropriate. Prepare clean gloves, the correct medication, water-
soluble lubricant and supplies. The source adult position is left
side-lying with the upper leg flexed, with minimal necessary exposure
and protected linens. A comfortable position and relaxation reduce
avoidable trauma; they do not remove the need to reassess consent or
contraindications.

149
01:01:41,208 --> 01:02:16,656
The source sequence is inspection, removal of the wrapper, water-
soluble lubrication, gentle insertion toward the umbilicus while the
patient relaxes, and placement against rectal mucosa rather than
stool. Its adult example is about five centimeters beyond the anal
sphincter; that is a source technique, not a universal depth for every
age, formulation or device. Do not force against a clenched sphincter
or substitute petroleum lubricant without product approval. Actual
product instructions and the supervised procedure control the method.

150
01:02:16,656 --> 01:02:46,584
The source preparation includes opportunity to empty the bladder,
explanation and privacy, a comfortable supine position with knees
flexed, minimal exposure, clean gloves and assessment for discharge,
itching or burning. Verify that the patient understands the proposed
contact and wishes to continue. Adapt positioning and assistance to
mobility, pain and dignity; a printed position is not an instruction
to restrain or force a person into it.

151
01:02:46,584 --> 01:03:21,792
The source removes packaging, uses water-soluble lubricant for the
specified suppository, and gently places it along the posterior
vaginal wall; its adult manual example is eight-to-ten centimeters. An
applicator follows its own manufacturer directions. This is an
explanation of the nursing example, not one depth or lubricant rule
for every patient/product. Afterward provide comfort, remove excess
lubricant as appropriate, discard supplies, clean hands and document
tolerance and relevant findings.

152
01:03:21,792 --> 01:04:02,352
Current British Columbia Emergency Health Services procedure P R
twenty-four is headed ACP and higher and requires CliniCall before
establishing or using the medication site. Community-paramedicine
pathway four point twenty-one exempts its trained community paramedics
from calling before insertion, within the service request and care
plan; it does not grant all PCPs independent medication authority. For
a PCP student, distinguish observing, explaining, supervised rehearsal
and assisting within actual authorization from independently inserting
or administering. A school reading label alone does not change that
distinction.

153
01:04:02,352 --> 01:04:36,624
PR24 organizes preparation around consent/plan, clean hands and
equipment, a healthy accessible site, skin antisepsis allowed to dry,
and the correct catheter/connector. Its selected device example primes
with medication, including an additional zero point four milliliters
for that set and connector; it is not an instruction to add that
volume to every device or flush any existing line. Prime/dead-space
volume, concentration and the ordered patient amount are different
quantities.

154
01:04:36,624 --> 01:05:07,656
Original comfort-care case: a patient cannot swallow the usual tablet,
is newly breathless and has a painful swollen site under a dressing. A
relative calls the device a butterfly and asks you to flush it and
give the usual medicine another way. Explain the immediate assessment,
what identity/medication/plan facts are missing, and how to obtain
appropriate help. The word palliative does not make hypoxia, pain or a
damaged medication site unimportant.

155
01:05:07,656 --> 01:05:44,520
Worked reasoning: assess airway, breathing, circulation, symptoms and
goals; identify the real device and site, review the medication record
and obtain the relevant clinician/consultation support. Do not crush a
tablet into an unverified route or flush an unknown medication-filled
set. Explain what you can do now for comfort and safety, preserve
privacy and document the observed problem, actual actions and
response. A clear handover connects route safety with compassionate
care rather than treating them as competing priorities.

156
01:05:44,520 --> 01:06:00,024
Medical assistance in dying is a distinct, regulated process. Under
current federal information, assessment and provision are performed by
physicians or nurse practitioners; it is not limited to people whose
death is reasonably foreseeable.

157
01:06:00,024 --> 01:06:22,056
As of October 2026, eligibility where mental illness is the sole
underlying condition remains excluded until March 17, 2027. Responders
should not assess eligibility, administer MAiD medication or rely on
an older BCEHS page for current legal details; follow current EMALB
and service policy.

158
01:06:22,056 --> 01:06:34,920
A person asking about MAiD is not automatically refusing every
assessment or symptom treatment. Ask what the current request means
and distinguish an information request from an urgent medical change.

159
01:06:34,920 --> 01:06:48,264
The dated federal eligibility note in the preceding slide is not a
student eligibility checklist. Recognize that qualified clinicians
govern that process; do not offer a conclusion about whether this
patient qualifies.

160
01:06:48,264 --> 01:07:03,576
If called during an unexpected deterioration, communicate the
confirmed event, current findings, relevant documents and known
treating-team plan. Do not assume that the word MAiD supplies an order
or explains an unknown medication event.

161
01:07:03,576 --> 01:07:19,512
Maintain a respectful, nonjudgmental interaction and obtain current
clinical direction for the actual problem. This is role recognition
and communication, not training to provide MAiD or an instruction to
improvise medication assistance.

162
01:07:19,512 --> 01:07:50,208
Distinguish symptom-focused palliative treatment from MAiD by the
intended intervention and authorized process, not by whether a
medicine can also cause sedation. MAiD includes clinician
administration and the separate prescribed/provided self-
administration method; qualified physicians or nurse practitioners
assess eligibility and provide it under the legal framework. A
paramedic listening to a question or supporting an authorized
transport is not thereby an assessor or provider of MAiD.

163
01:07:50,208 --> 01:08:28,080
The Emergency Medical Assistants Licensing Board policy twenty
nineteen dash zero one applies to qualified EMAs licensed or endorsed
to initiate intravenous lines. It allows an ordered IV start for MAiD
from a physician or nurse practitioner, but prohibits EMA
administration of MAiD medication in any circumstance. Licence,
training, the order and service policy still matter. Its 2026 change-
index entry updates contact information, not this authority. The
policy body, rather than merely its index title, was reviewed.

164
01:08:28,080 --> 01:08:59,352
After death, dignity, family support, identity, belongings, cultural
wishes and the appropriate notification pathway still matter. Algor
mortis means postmortem cooling. Rigor mortis means stiffening.
Lividity means dependent blood pooling. Pallor means paleness. The
source’s statement that skin develops passive euthanasia is a wording
error, not a physical sign. These signs cannot, by themselves,
determine an exact time or cause of death.

165
01:08:59,352 --> 01:09:31,992
The hospital nursing description of washing, removing tubes or
preparing viewing is not a blanket prehospital instruction. In a
potentially reportable BC death, protect the body, immediate
environment and relevant evidence, follow the service and coroner
requirements and obtain required authorization before alteration.
Explain constraints compassionately and preserve privacy. Do not
remove lines merely for tidiness. Do not clean the scene or assume
that a funeral transfer is authorized.

166
01:09:31,992 --> 01:09:43,056
Use an observer to note whether the learner explained their role,
asked before touch, used plain language, invited the patient’s own
account and reassessed when the scene changed.

167
01:09:43,056 --> 01:10:02,136
For a refusal station, verbalize the decision-specific facts, support
provided, risks and options discussed, consultation, documentation and
safety-net. For a crisis station, verbalize exit, medical
differential, monitoring plan and stop point.

168
01:10:02,136 --> 01:10:16,704
A useful handover separates the patient’s words from your
observations. Include changes over time, the capacity or refusal
discussion when relevant, the patient’s goal, applicable documents and
any safety concerns.

169
01:10:16,704 --> 01:10:46,272
Use SBAR to organize a consultation request. For a current BCEHS care
transition, A zero three specifies ATMIST AMBO. Carry forward the
actual findings, trend, patient goals, relevant consent discussion and
safety concern in the appropriate format. Name the next task and
confirm who accepted responsibility. Do not leave only an unexplained
not-for-hospital label or an incomplete monitoring plan.

170
01:10:46,272 --> 01:10:59,808
In the palliative case, begin with new breathlessness and the
patient’s goal of relief while remaining with the partner. Do not
start and end with not for hospital; that phrase hides the clinical
question.

171
01:10:59,808 --> 01:11:12,216
Say which document was actually reviewed or which preference was
reported, then describe assessment and response. Distinguish a
contacted team from a team that has accepted responsibility.

172
01:11:12,216 --> 01:11:28,632
For the refusal case, carry forward the specific decision,
information/support given, observed understanding and the current
consultation or plan. For the crisis case, include safety changes and
physiological trend, not only the behaviour score.

173
01:11:28,632 --> 01:11:42,624
Ask for acknowledgment of the next responsibility. A handover is
complete when the receiving person understands the unresolved concern
and the actual next action, not simply when the speaker finishes a
mnemonic.

174
01:11:42,624 --> 01:12:13,848
Original transfer round: a patient being moved between facilities has
a confirmed maintenance plan and received a medicine at the sending
ward. During loading the patient becomes unusually quiet, develops
shallow breathing and cannot explain new abdominal pain. A companion
wants the next planned medication given immediately and asks to bypass
the receiving hospital because a palliative document is in the bag.
Identify which assumptions have stopped being safe and which facts you
need now.

175
01:12:13,848 --> 01:12:51,792
Worked reasoning: pause the routine transfer script and assess airway,
ventilation, circulation, responsiveness and the new symptom;
coordinate appropriate support. Confirm the actual medicine, dose,
route, time and response, and re-conference the plan that no longer
matches the patient. Verify the document’s real instruction and the
person’s goals through the applicable pathway; neither a companion’s
request nor a palliative label establishes informed refusal or drug
authority. Communicate the change and the agreed next action with
explicit ownership.

176
01:12:51,792 --> 01:13:05,136
After a rehearsal, compare the decision path with the evidence
available at that moment. A good outcome does not prove that the
reasoning was sound, and a difficult outcome does not by itself prove
the learner acted badly.

177
01:13:05,136 --> 01:13:16,272
Ask for specific instructor feedback on communication, assessment,
monitoring and scope. A discussion does not replace a school checkoff
or real-world authorization.

178
01:13:16,272 --> 01:13:27,480
Begin the debrief with the evidence available at the time, including
what was not known. Do not award good reasoning only because the
observer later revealed a favourable ending.

179
01:13:27,480 --> 01:13:42,504
Choose one concrete repair. If the patient’s refusal was never
clarified, repeat the first conversation with a question about the
specific proposal. If a quiet patient was assumed stable, repeat the
response to that change.

180
01:13:42,504 --> 01:13:53,640
The observer writes the actual improved action and remaining gap. Keep
a useful behaviour as well as one change; confidence alone is not the
performance measure.

181
01:13:53,640 --> 01:14:05,976
Bring unresolved skill or legal-policy questions to supervised
teaching. This round creates a practice record and a next learning
task, not a declaration that the school station has been passed.
